DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     These functions provide calendar arithmetic for a large range of years,
     starting at March 1st, year zero (i.e., 1 B.C.) and ending way beyond
     year 100000.

     Programs should be linked with --llccaalleennddaarr.

     The functions eeaasstteerrgg(), eeaasstteerroogg() and eeaasstteerroojj() store the date of
     Easter Sunday into the structure pointed at by _d_t and return a pointer to
     this structure.  The function eeaasstteerrgg() assumes Gregorian Calendar
     (adopted by most western churches after 1582) and the functions
     eeaasstteerroogg() and eeaasstteerroojj() compute the date of Easter Sunday according to
     the orthodox rules (Western churches before 1582, Greek and Russian Or‐
     thodox Church until today).  The result returned by eeaasstteerroogg() is the
     date in Gregorian Calendar, whereas eeaasstteerroojj() returns the date in Julian
     Calendar.

     The functions ggddaattee(), jjddaattee(), nnddaayyssgg() and nnddaayyssjj() provide conversions
     between the common "year, month, day" notation of a date and the "number
     of days" representation, which is better suited for calculations.  The
     days are numbered from March 1st year 1 B.C., starting with zero, so the
     number of a day gives the number of days since March 1st, year 1 B.C.
     The conversions work for nonnegative day numbers only.

     The ggddaattee() and jjddaattee() functions store the date corresponding to the day
     number _n_d into the structure pointed at by _d_t and return a pointer to
     this structure.

     The nnddaayyssgg() and nnddaayyssjj() functions return the day number of the date
     pointed at by _d_t.

     The ggddaattee() and nnddaayyssgg() functions assume Gregorian Calendar after Octo‐
     ber 4, 1582 and Julian Calendar before, whereas jjddaattee() and nnddaayyssjj() as‐
     sume Julian Calendar throughout.

     The two calendars differ by the definition of the leap year.  The Julian
     Calendar says every year that is a multiple of four is a leap year.  The
     Gregorian Calendar excludes years that are multiples of 100 and not mul‐
     tiples of 400.  This means the years 1700, 1800, 1900, 2100 are not leap
     years and the year 2000 is a leap year.  The new rules were inaugurated
     on October 4, 1582 by deleting ten days following this date.  Most
     catholic countries adopted the new calendar by the end of the 16th cen‐
     tury, whereas others stayed with the Julian Calendar until the 20th cen‐
     tury.  The United Kingdom and their colonies switched on September 2,
     1752.  They already had to delete 11 days.

     The function wweeeekk() returns the number of the week which contains the day
     numbered _n_d.  The argument _*_y_e_a_r is set with the year that contains (the
     greater part of) the week.  The weeks are numbered per year starting with
     week 1, which is the first week in a year that includes more than three
     days of the year.  Weeks start on Monday.  This function is defined for
     Gregorian Calendar only.

     The function wweeeekkddaayy() returns the weekday (Mo = 0 .. Su = 6) of the day
     numbered _n_d.

     The structure _d_a_t_e is defined in <_c_a_l_e_n_d_a_r_._h>.  It contains these fields:

           int y;          /∗ year (0000 - ????) ∗/
           int m;          /∗ month (1 - 12) ∗/
           int d;          /∗ day of month (1 - 31) ∗/

     The year zero is written as "1 B.C." by historians and "0" by astronomers
     and in this library.

